In readNormalIncomplete, read only the bases a loaded row holds (libs/axf/refseq.c).

While an external reference is still loading in the background, readNormalIncomplete unpacks
each row the loader has finished with getBases_2na, always max_seq_len bases from the row's
start. The last row is usually shorter, so once the loader has marked every row loaded (it
does so just before it switches readers to readNormal), a read of the last row unpacks up to
max_seq_len / 4 bytes past the end of the reference's packed bases. That segfaults when the
allocation ends just before an unmapped page, as glibc's mmap'd large allocations can.
Upstream: https://github.com/ncbi/ncbi-vdb/pull/289 (this hunk is that commit, rebased onto
3.3.0). Drop this patch once the vendored ncbi-vdb includes it.

--- a/libs/axf/refseq.c
+++ b/libs/axf/refseq.c
@@ -250,7 +250,10 @@
         for (row = first; row <= last && rc == 0; ++row) {
             ++async->hits;
             if (rowIsLoaded(async, row)) {
-                getBases_2na(buf, rowToPosition(async, row), async->max_seq_len, self->bases, &self->Ns);
+                /* the last row can be shorter than max_seq_len; don't read past the end of bases */
+                unsigned const rowStart = rowToPosition(async, row);
+                unsigned const rowLen = length - rowStart < async->max_seq_len ? length - rowStart : async->max_seq_len;
+                getBases_2na(buf, rowStart, rowLen, self->bases, &self->Ns);
             }
             else {
                 ReadStringResult read = { NULL, 0 };
